    {
        "content": "<p>Hi folks,</p>\n<p>I am wondering what the defining property for Leibniz algebras should be if we wish to include them in Mathlib at some point. The two options are:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">left_leibniz'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">right_leibniz'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>One argument for \"left\" is that the Lie algebras in Mathlib already have Leibniz from the left, and that Mathlib seems to favor left actions. (Cf. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RingTheory/Derivation/Basic.html#Derivation.leibniz\">Derivation.leibniz</a>, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Lie/Basic.html#leibniz_lie\">leibniz_lie</a>)</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">leibniz_lie</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆⁆</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>One argument for \"right\" is that the literature vastly favors right-Leibniz algebras (so this would greatly facilitate some formalization efforts), and historically the axiom is written from the right.</p>\n<p>What do you think?</p>",
        "id": 598438796,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Schwahn",
        "timestamp": 1780010342
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>/poll Left or right?<br>\nLeft<br>\nRight<br>\nWe should have both</p>",
        "id": 598438825,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Schwahn",
        "timestamp": 1780010364
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe we could have a tool that automatically converts between the conventions like <code>@[to_additive]</code>?</p>",
        "id": 598464618,
        "sender_full_name": "Jack McCarthy",
        "timestamp": 1780024285
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think one consideration when choosing this is to think about your <a href=\"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/The-Simplifier/Simp-Normal-Forms/#--tech-term-simp-normal-form\">simp normal form</a> . </p>\n<p>This may not make you decide whether or not to have both, but I think it will form part of the consideration as to which you focus on, and what things you tag as <code>simp</code> , as well as the interaction with whatever is already tagged (including meshing with that potential mathlib preference you noted).</p>",
        "id": 598470266,
        "sender_full_name": "Yan Yablonovskiy 🇺🇦",
        "timestamp": 1780028811
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree, we need to pick one for <code>simp</code>. Similar to the <code>lt ≤</code> vs <code>gt ≥</code> debate.</p>\n<p>Unfortunately, I do not know much about Leibniz algebras, but I trust Paul when he says that the literature vastly favors the right. If that's the case, \"right\" should be the normal form.</p>",
        "id": 598475468,
        "sender_full_name": "Jack McCarthy",
        "timestamp": 1780031305
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am not sure if I understand what you mean by \"picking one for <code>simp</code>\". I don't think the Leibniz property should be a simp lemma; not least because the RHS of <code>left_leibniz'</code> contains a term of the same form of the LHS.</p>\n<p>One thing I should maybe have been more clear on: <code>left_leibniz'</code> and <code>right_leibniz'</code> are NOT equivalent; so this is a design choice, not an implementation detail. It is, of course, the case that if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> is a left Leibniz algebra, then the algebra <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{\\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathrm mtight\">op</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (with reversed multiplication) is a right Leibniz algebra, so their theories are equivalent.</p>",
        "id": 599193249,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Schwahn",
        "timestamp": 1780328574
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>One thing possibly worth considering is whether Leibniz algebras should be a <code>mul</code> or a <code>bracket</code>. In the latter case, it might make sense to go with the left convention by default: <br>\nBrackets are defined as <code>bracket : L → M → M</code>.  So one could refine the current construction of <code>LieRing</code> as something like:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LeibnizAction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Bracket</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bracket</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">lie_add</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">left_leibniz'</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆⁆</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LeftLeibnizAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Bracket</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LeibnizAction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">add_lie</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LieRing'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Bracket</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LeftLeibnizAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">lie_self</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⁅</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">⁆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 609784942,
        "sender_full_name": "Leonid Ryvkin",
        "timestamp": 1783930518
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's a good point. Currently we're going with <code>Mul</code>, for a few reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>We have converged on implementing Leibniz algebras as <em>right</em> Leibniz algebras since the literature favors them. This would clash with the <code>leibniz_lie</code> axiom of Lie rings.</li>\n<li>Our goal is to attack classifications of arbitrary (non-associative, non-unital) algebras in low dimensions, where some happen to be Leibniz and others not, and then its good to have a typeclass <code>IsLeibniz</code> extending <code>NonAssocRing</code>.</li>\n<li>There is no scenario I can think of where one would want to apply theorems about Leibniz algebras to a Lie algebra, as the statements will always become either trivial or weaker than the corresponding Lie algebra statement. For example, defining the derived series and lower central series for Lie algebras is quite easy because there is a <code>Bracket</code> structure on <code>LieIdeal</code>s. Leibniz algebras also have derived and lower central series, but they need to be defined in a more ad hoc way because there is no such <code>Bracket</code> structure (you can take powers of an ideal, but not multiply two ideals to get another one). Therefore I doubt the advantages of having <code>LieRing</code> as an extension of Leibniz algebras.</li>\n</ol>",
        "id": 611073252,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Schwahn",
        "timestamp": 1784209063
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'll ping <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"240862\">@Oliver Nash</span> here who might have opinions</p>",
        "id": 611081996,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1784211227
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't have as much time to comment as I'd like to I'll just quickly remark that the main reasons for using the <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Bracket#doc\">docs#Bracket</a> for Lie algebras are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Alignment with informal literature</li>\n<li>Permit support for situations like Poisson algebras where we have two products at the same time, we need both <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Mul#doc\">docs#Mul</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Bracket#doc\">docs#Bracket</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p>I don't know how much these apply in the case of Leibniz algebras. I'll try to return later to consider the left / right issue but there's no need to wait for any input I may have to offer.</p>",
        "id": 611097350,
        "sender_full_name": "Oliver Nash",
        "timestamp": 1784214714
    }
]